-- title:
--   Discreteness of the Fuchsian group of a rational quaternion order
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ be nonzero and let $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ be the associated rational quaternion algebra. Let $R$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ satisfying `IsOrder`, that is: $1\in R$; $R$ is closed under multiplication; the $\mathbb{Q}$-span of $R$ is all of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$; and $R$ is finitely generated as a $\mathbb{Z}$-module. Let $\iota\colon \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\to M_2(\mathbb{R})$ be an injective homomorphism of $\mathbb{Q}$-algebras. Write `unitGroup R` for the subgroup of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]^{\times}$ generated by those units $u$ whose underlying quaternion satisfies the predicate `IsUnitOf R`, and let `fuchsianGroup R ι` be the subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ obtained as the intersection of the image of `unitGroup R` under the map on unit groups induced by $\iota$ with the kernel of the determinant $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})\to\mathbb{R}^{\times}$, i.e. the elements $\iota(u)$ of determinant $1$. The assertion is that `fuchsianGroup R ι`, with the topology induced from $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, is discrete.
--
--   This is the classical statement that the norm-one (determinant-one) units of an order in a rational quaternion algebra, embedded in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, form a Fuchsian group, the discreteness being the first step towards the quaternionic Shimura curves used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld setting. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.fuchsianGroup_discrete_neg_mem_and_exists_isCompact`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.fuchsianGroup_discrete_neg_mem_and_exists_isCompact), which packages discreteness together with further properties of this subgroup.
